<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="A reproducible public analysis of {{ metadata.row_count }} responses to the Forever Feral Community Feedback survey.">
  <title>Forever Feral Community Feedback</title>
  <style>
    :root { --ink:#17212b; --muted:#5d6873; --paper:#f7f5ef; --card:#fff; --green:#1b7f79; --red:#b4474c; --blue:#345f88; --line:#d9d7cf; }
    * { box-sizing:border-box; }
    html { scroll-behavior:smooth; }
    body { margin:0; color:var(--ink); background:var(--paper); font-family:Inter,ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif; line-height:1.62; }
    header { color:white; background:linear-gradient(135deg,#173a34,#315b50 65%,#84672f); padding:4.8rem 1.2rem 4rem; }
    .wrap { width:min(1120px,calc(100% - 2rem)); margin:auto; }
    header .wrap { width:min(920px,calc(100% - 2rem)); }
    .eyebrow { letter-spacing:.12em; text-transform:uppercase; font-size:.78rem; font-weight:800; color:#d9c995; }
    h1 { font-family:Georgia,serif; font-size:clamp(2.45rem,7vw,5.2rem); line-height:1.02; margin:.5rem 0 1rem; max-width:13ch; }
    .deck { font-size:clamp(1.08rem,2.2vw,1.35rem); max-width:720px; color:#edf4f1; }
    nav { position:sticky; top:0; z-index:4; background:rgba(247,245,239,.95); border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); backdrop-filter:blur(10px); overflow-x:auto; white-space:nowrap; }
    nav .wrap { display:flex; gap:1.25rem; padding:.75rem 0; }
    nav a { color:#31443f; font-size:.88rem; font-weight:700; text-decoration:none; }
    main { padding:2rem 0 5rem; }
    section { margin:4.2rem 0; scroll-margin-top:4rem; }
    h2 { font-family:Georgia,serif; font-size:clamp(1.8rem,4vw,2.75rem); line-height:1.15; margin:0 0 .8rem; }
    h3 { margin:2.2rem 0 .55rem; }
    p, li { max-width:78ch; }
    .lead { font-size:1.18rem; color:#33413e; }
    .cards { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr); gap:1rem; margin:1.8rem 0; }
    .card { background:var(--card); border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:14px; padding:1.15rem; box-shadow:0 5px 20px rgba(20,40,32,.04); }
    .card strong { display:block; font-size:1.75rem; line-height:1.1; color:#214f46; }
    .card span { display:block; color:var(--muted); font-size:.9rem; margin-top:.35rem; }
    .finding { background:#e8f1ee; border-left:5px solid var(--green); border-radius:4px 12px 12px 4px; padding:1.2rem 1.35rem; margin:1.5rem 0; }
    .caveat { background:#f4ead9; border-left-color:#a97926; }
    figure { background:white; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:14px; padding:1rem; margin:1.4rem 0 2.4rem; overflow:hidden; }
    figure img { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; }
    figcaption { color:var(--muted); font-size:.86rem; margin:.6rem .3rem 0; }
    .grid2 { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:1.2rem; }
    .table-scroll { overflow:auto; background:white; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:12px; margin:1rem 0 2rem; }
    .data-table { width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:.87rem; }
    .data-table th { text-align:left; background:#eef1ed; position:sticky; top:0; }
    .data-table th,.data-table td { padding:.65rem .75rem; border-bottom:1px solid #e5e5df; vertical-align:top; }
    .download { display:inline-block; margin:.2rem .35rem .2rem 0; color:#245b7a; font-weight:700; }
    blockquote { margin:1rem 0; padding:1rem 1.2rem; background:#fff; border-left:4px solid #6c5aa8; border-radius:4px 10px 10px 4px; }
    code { font-size:.88em; background:#ecebe5; padding:.1rem .3rem; border-radius:4px; }
    footer { border-top:1px solid var(--line); color:var(--muted); padding:2rem 0 4rem; font-size:.9rem; }
    @media (max-width:800px) { .cards { grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; } .grid2 { grid-template-columns:1fr; } header { padding-top:3rem; } }
    @media (max-width:480px) { .cards { grid-template-columns:1fr; } figure { padding:.35rem; margin-left:-.35rem; margin-right:-.35rem; } .wrap { width:min(100% - 1.2rem,1120px); } }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<header><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Community survey · independent analysis</div>
  <h1>Forever Feral feedback</h1>
  <p class="deck">What this engaged Feral community likes, dislikes, and wants changed about the currently revealed design.</p>
</div></header>
<nav aria-label="Report sections"><div class="wrap">
  <a href="#summary">Summary</a><a href="#sample">Sample</a><a href="#headline">Headline results</a>
  <a href="#powershifting">Powershifting</a><a href="#mechanics">Mechanics</a><a href="#subgroups">Subgroups</a>
  <a href="#text">Free response</a><a href="#methods">Methods</a><a href="#downloads">Downloads</a>
</div></nav>
<main class="wrap">
<section id="summary">
  <h2>Executive summary</h2>
  <p class="lead">The updated data supports a nuanced central finding: the strongest rejection is aimed at the apparent loss of activity, resource agency, mastery, and shapeshifting identity—not a single unanimous demand for one historical mechanic.</p>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Negative toward current direction"]) }}%</strong><span>negative toward the current direction</span></div>
    <div class="card"><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Less excited than Classic"]) }}%</strong><span>less excited than for Classic Feral</span></div>
    <div class="card"><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Functional replacement acceptable"]) }}%</strong><span>accept a functional replacement for literal powershifting</span></div>
    <div class="card"><strong>{{ "%.1f"|format(current["Specifically want Classic powershifting restored"]) }}%</strong><span>specifically ask for Classic-style powershifting</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="finding"><strong>What the evidence supports.</strong> Most respondents do not insist on literal powershifting, but that does not mean its removal is well received. The revealed implementation is widely seen as too slow, passive, or shallow. An alternative has room to succeed if it restores activity, meaningful resource conversion, moment-to-moment decisions, mastery, and a recognizably shapeshifting identity.</div>
  <p>Positive findings are real and should not be lost: combo points on the player, Clearcasting/Omen, Tiger’s Fury, and a stronger bleed identity all draw more positive than negative reaction. Respondents often approve of a broad direction while questioning its current execution—for example, liking bleeds as an identity but not finding the revealed bleed emphasis active enough.</p>
</section>

<section id="sample">
  <h2>Sample and data quality</h2>
  <p>The analysis covers <strong>{{ metadata.row_count }} valid responses</strong> collected from {{ collection_start }} through {{ collection_end }}. A valid response is a row with a populated timestamp; allocated but empty sheet rows are not counted.</p>
  <div class="finding caveat"><strong>Who this represents.</strong> This is a self-selected sample of engaged Feral players, not a probability sample of all current or potential Forever players. Descriptive percentages should not be generalized to the entire player base without that caveat.</div>
  <p>The sample is unusually informed: {{ sample_counts.familiarity["I have played the current test build"] }} respondents played the current test build, {{ sample_counts.familiarity["I have closely reviewed talents, abilities, footage, or theorycrafting"] }} closely reviewed source material or theorycrafting, and {{ sample_counts.familiarity["I have followed detailed community discussion"] }} followed detailed community discussion. Its largest primary-interest groups are Cat DPS ({{ sample_counts.primary_interest["Cat DPS in organized PvE"] }}), hybrid/off-tank play ({{ sample_counts.primary_interest["Hybrid Cat/Bear or off-tank play"] }}), and Bear tanking ({{ sample_counts.primary_interest["Bear tank in organized PvE"] }}). Experience is concentrated among high-end/competitive ({{ sample_counts.experience["High-end or competitive Feral player"] }}) and regular organized-raiding players ({{ sample_counts.experience["Regular organized-raiding Feral player"] }}).</p>
  <p>The duplicate audit found {{ metadata.exact_duplicate_rows }} rows in exact duplicate-answer groups. {{ metadata.duplicate_timestamp_rows }} rows share a timestamp with another row; timestamp collisions alone were not treated as duplicates because their answers differ. Missingness is reported per question below.</p>
  <details><summary>Missingness by question</summary><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.missingness|safe }}</div></details>
  <p><a class="download" href="tables/familiarity.csv">Familiarity CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/primary_interest.csv">Primary interest CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/experience.csv">Experience CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/versions_played.csv">Versions played CSV</a></p>
</section>

<section id="headline">
  <h2>Headline structured results</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <figure><img src="figures/overall-direction.svg" alt="Horizontal bar chart comparing negative, positive, mixed or neutral, and insufficient-information reactions to the current direction."><figcaption>All categories retain the full denominator; “not enough information” is never silently dropped.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src="figures/likelihood-to-play.svg" alt="Horizontal bar chart of likelihood to regularly play Feral, including would play, unsure, would not play, and not applicable."><figcaption>Intent to play is less negative than broad sentiment, but substantial uncertainty remains.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <figure><img src="figures/excitement.svg" alt="Horizontal bar chart showing excitement relative to Classic Feral, including more, equal, less, and insufficient information."><figcaption>Comparison with Classic Feral is markedly negative in this engaged sample.</figcaption></figure>
</section>

<section id="powershifting">
  <h2>Powershifting nuance</h2>
  <p>Respondents separate the historical mechanic from the properties it supplied. {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Functional replacement acceptable"]) }}% do not require literal powershifting if another mechanic supplies comparable activity and resource decisions; {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Prefer non-powershifting if engaging"]) }}% affirmatively prefer a non-powershifting design if it retains depth. Only {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Specifically want Classic powershifting restored"]) }}% choose literal restoration as their closest view.</p>
  <p>At the same time, {{ "%.1f"|format(current["Negative reaction to Furor/loss of powershifting"]) }}% react negatively to Furor and the loss or reduction of powershifting. Read together, these answers reject the inference that openness to alternatives equals approval of the current replacement.</p>
  <figure><img src="figures/powershifting-preference.svg" alt="Horizontal bar chart comparing functional replacement, engaging non-powershifting, literal Classic restoration, slower rotation, no preference, and insufficient information."><figcaption>These are mutually exclusive Q8 choices (n={{ metadata.row_count }}).</figcaption></figure>
  <p>Q15 topic assignments involving powershifting were separately polarity-coded because topic models are weak at negation. “Preserve powershifting” and “preserve its removal” are not treated as the same conclusion.</p>
  <details><summary>Q15 powershifting polarity table</summary><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.q15_powershift_polarity|safe }}</div></details>
</section>

<section id="mechanics">
  <h2>What respondents value—and where the design falls short</h2>
  <figure><img src="figures/gameplay-qualities.svg" alt="Horizontal bars ranking gameplay qualities by the share calling each essential or very important."><figcaption>Full five-level distributions are in the downloadable matrix table.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src="figures/mechanic-reactions.svg" alt="Diverging bar chart comparing positive and negative reaction to ten revealed mechanics."><figcaption>Neutral and not-enough-information responses remain in the denominator and are available in the full table.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>The strongest positives are combo points stored on the player and Clearcasting/Omen. Tiger’s Fury and bleed emphasis are also net positive. The sharpest negatives concern Furor/powershifting, energy generation and pooling, AoE/Swipe, and weaving possibilities. The current design also receives broad disagreement that it preserves Feral’s shapeshifting identity or provides enough engaging decisions, appropriate downtime, and room for mastery.</p>
  <figure><img src="figures/developer-attention.svg" alt="Horizontal bars showing the ten most selected areas for developer attention."><figcaption>Q12 allowed up to three selections; percentages can legitimately sum above 100%.</figcaption></figure>
  <p><a class="download" href="tables/importance_matrix.csv">Importance matrix CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/agreement_matrix.csv">Agreement matrix CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/mechanic_reactions.csv">Mechanic reactions CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/developer_attention.csv">Attention selections CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/directions_to_test.csv">Test directions CSV</a></p>
</section>

<section id="subgroups">
  <h2>Subgroup comparisons</h2>
  <p>Differences below are descriptive, not causal. Every displayed subgroup has at least 30 respondents; each major proportion includes a Wilson 95% confidence interval. Overlapping intervals and self-selection limit what can be inferred.</p>
  <figure><img src="figures/subgroups.svg" alt="Dot and interval plot of negative-direction percentages across gameplay-interest, experience, familiarity, and powershifting-preference subgroups."><figcaption>Denominators appear in labels. The downloadable table also includes likelihood to play and excitement outcomes.</figcaption></figure>
  <p><a class="download" href="tables/subgroups.csv">All subgroup estimates CSV</a></p>
</section>

<section id="text">
  <h2>Free-response themes</h2>
  <p>Word clouds size each term by the number of distinct responses mentioning it, not by raw repetition. Survey boilerplate is removed and meaningful phrases—including “Tiger’s Fury,” “energy pooling,” “skill expression,” “Bear weaving,” and “raid utility”—are preserved.</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q14.png" alt="Word cloud for what developers should understand, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q14: what developers should understand.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q15.png" alt="Word cloud for what should be preserved, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q15: what should be preserved.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q16.png" alt="Word cloud for the one desired mechanic or design change, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q16: one requested change and intended experience.</figcaption></figure>
  <h3>Transparent thematic validation</h3>
  <p>Rule-based mention coding checks the model against 14 predeclared themes. Categories are non-exclusive and ambiguous wording is not forced into a directional position. This layer distinguishes literal restoration, a functional equivalent, and non-powershifting-but-engaging requests from related complaints about energy, APM, mastery, identity, weaving, bleeds, AoE, hybrid play, Rogue similarity, tuning, and raid utility.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.thematic_validation|safe }}</div>
  <h3>Topic models</h3>
  <p>Q14, Q15, and Q16 are modeled separately with TF-IDF unigrams/bigrams and non-negative matrix factorization. Candidate solutions from 4–10 topics were compared for stability, term diversity, redundancy, and reconstruction error. Labels were assigned only after inspecting top terms and high-weight responses. Primary-topic shares are an interpretive summary—not survey vote percentages—and the downloadable tables retain soft weights and secondary topics.</p>
  <figure><img src="figures/topic-prevalence.svg" alt="Horizontal bars showing exclusive primary-topic shares for separately modeled Q14, Q15, and Q16 responses."><figcaption>Each response receives one primary topic for this chart; many responses mix themes.</figcaption></figure>
  <p><a class="download" href="tables/topic_prevalence.csv">Topic prevalence CSV</a><a class="download" href="tables/topic_terms_q14.csv">Q14 terms</a><a class="download" href="tables/topic_terms_q15.csv">Q15 terms</a><a class="download" href="tables/topic_terms_q16.csv">Q16 terms</a><a class="download" href="tables/topic_assignments_q14.csv">Q14 soft weights</a></p>
  <h3>Anonymous representative excerpts</h3>
  {% if tables.anonymous_excerpts|length %}
    {% for _, row in tables.anonymous_excerpts.iterrows() %}<blockquote>“{{ row.excerpt }}” <small>— {{ row.question }}, quoted with permission</small></blockquote>{% endfor %}
  {% else %}<p>No excerpts were published in this build pending final manual privacy review.</p>{% endif %}
</section>

<section id="change">
  <h2>Change from the earlier 675-response snapshot</h2>
  <p>The additional responses do not materially overturn the earlier conclusions. Percentage-point changes are shown below; the live snapshot, rather than the earlier figures, is authoritative for this report.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.comparison_675|safe }}</div>
  <p><a class="download" href="tables/comparison_675.csv">Comparison CSV</a></p>
</section>

<section id="methods">
  <h2>Methodology and limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Source.</strong> Public CSV export of spreadsheet <code>{{ metadata.source_spreadsheet_id }}</code>, worksheet <code>{{ metadata.worksheet }}</code>, retrieved {{ retrieved_display }}.</li>
    <li><strong>Snapshot.</strong> {{ metadata.row_count }} populated timestamp rows × {{ metadata.column_count }} columns; SHA-256 <code>{{ metadata.sha256 }}</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Denominators.</strong> Counts and percentages retain “not enough information.” Multiselect tables clearly permit totals above 100%.</li>
    <li><strong>Uncertainty.</strong> Wilson 95% intervals are shown for major subgroup proportions. They quantify sampling variability conditional on this sample, not representativeness.</li>
    <li><strong>Qualitative analysis.</strong> Fixed random seed {{ metadata.random_seed }}. NMF topics are descriptive summaries; negation and mixed statements require polarity checks and human review.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy.</strong> Raw responses are ignored by Git. Published excerpts require explicit Q17 permission and manual identifier screening. Aggregate-only responses are never quoted.</li>
    <li><strong>Limitations.</strong> Self-selection, community-channel reach, response dependence, question wording, and the still-evolving revealed build all constrain interpretation. Subgroup differences are not causal effects.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="downloads">
  <h2>Downloadable aggregate tables</h2>
  <p>All public tables are generated by the same refresh command as this page.</p>
  {% for name in ["source_metadata.json","overall_direction.csv","likelihood_to_play.csv","excitement.csv","powershifting_preference.csv","developer_attention.csv","directions_to_test.csv","importance_matrix.csv","agreement_matrix.csv","mechanic_reactions.csv","subgroups.csv","thematic_validation.csv","q15_powershift_polarity.csv","topic_prevalence.csv","comparison_675.csv","wordcloud_q14_terms.csv","wordcloud_q15_terms.csv","wordcloud_q16_terms.csv"] %}
